      "events": [
        {
          "id": "1ac94f6926054d89",
          "label": "A1",
          "role": "A",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.9871821309399059,
          "seconds": 0.027185512396196525,
          "hypothesis": "Replacing insertion sort with a max-heap reduces O(k) shifts per point to O(log k) heap operations, improving memory locality by reducing writes and enabling better cache utilization for the small working set of size k. Additionally, using a frequency array instead of qsort avoids sorting overhead.",
          "reason": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "dfb84433121944fa",
          "label": "B1",
          "role": "B",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.9859185372739435,
          "seconds": 0.029903754281500976,
          "hypothesis": "Replacing insertion-sort-based top-k selection with a max-heap removes loop-carried dependencies in the inner shift loop, enabling compiler auto-vectorization of distance computation and reducing complexity from O(N*k) to O(N log k).",
          "reason": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "dbe95ff53cc34a0d",
          "label": "C1",
          "role": "C",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.37721174943232283,
          "seconds": 3.4567993907257915,
          "hypothesis": "Parallelizing the outer loop over groups with per-thread local top-k lists and static scheduling preserves input order within each chunk, enabling a deterministic merge that matches serial tie-breaking. This reduces thread synchronization overhead while exploiting OpenMP work sharing.",
          "reason": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "0cc032e176f14db0",
          "label": "A2",
          "role": "A",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.9856583404266092,
          "seconds": 0.030464354902505875,
          "hypothesis": "Flattening all points into contiguous struct-of-arrays (separate x, y, label arrays) improves memory locality by eliminating pointer indirection through groups and enabling sequential cache-friendly access. Combined with a max-heap for top-k selection (O(N log k)), this reduces both latency and bandwidth pressure compared to the original insertion-sort approach.",
          "reason": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "74722b26180e4b94",
          "label": "B2",
          "role": "B",
          "parent": "dfb84433121944fa",
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.9859630564525413,
          "seconds": 0.02980786624054114,
          "hypothesis": "Restructuring point storage from AoS (array of structs) to SoA (separate xs/ys arrays) and using ARM NEON SIMD intrinsics to compute squared Euclidean distances for four points simultaneously removes loop-carried dependencies in the distance computation, enabling vectorization. The heap insertion remains serial but is now separated into a helper function; overall runtime decreases because distance calculation dominates.",
          "reason": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "84f25041ddf54756",
          "label": "C2",
          "role": "C",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "build_failed",
          "score": 0.0,
          "seconds": null,
          "hypothesis": "Using OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling on the outer loop over groups, each thread maintains its own local sorted list of k nearest neighbors (allocated once outside the parallel region). After the parallel region, per-thread lists are merged in order of increasing thread ID to preserve input-order tie-breaking. This reduces synchronization overhead compared to critical sections while exploiting work sharing and static scheduling for deterministic chunk assignment.",
          "reason": "Build failed or exceeded candidate timeout"
        },
        {
          "id": "22be3c4db8d64ce4",
          "label": "A3",
          "role": "A",
          "parent": null,
          "sources": [],
          "state": "valid",
          "score": 0.9857884435099774,
          "seconds": 0.030184007560213406,
          "hypothesis": "Changing data layout from array-of-structs to struct-of-arrays for point coordinates within each group improves memory locality by enabling sequential access to x and y arrays separately, reducing cache misses. Combining this with a max-heap for top-k selection reduces the O(k) insertion cost to O(log k), further improving performance.",
          "reason": ""
